      },
      "rev_n_1_31460e_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_31460e_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_31460e",
        "thought_text": "Model the problem as finding the minimum sum of a subsequence with step k in a circular array of size n, then output the 1-based index of the start position that achieves this minimum (breaking ties by choosing the smallest index).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589620.9659576
      },
      "rev_n_2_17b625_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_17b625_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_17b625",
        "thought_text": "Since n is divisible by k, the array can be viewed as k independent chains of length n/k. We can compute the sum for each possible starting offset modulo k and track the global minimum and its corresponding starting index.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589620.9659715
      },
      "rev_n_3_b4c6db_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_b4c6db_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_b4c6db",
        "thought_text": "Implement a sliding window approach where we calculate the sum of every k-th element starting from each index i from 0 to k-1, keeping track of the minimum sum found and the earliest starting position.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589620.965979
      },
      "rev_n_4_a2b815_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_a2b815_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_a2b815",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through all possible starting positions from 1 to k, calculate the sum of powers for the specific sequence of tasks Dima performs (indices i, i+k, i+2k...), and select the starting position with the minimum sum.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8229990770438821770",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589646.329813
      },
      "rev_n_5_bab247_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_bab247_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_bab247",
        "thought_text": "Since the array is circular and the pattern repeats every k tasks, we can treat the problem as finding the minimum sum of a subsequence with step k in a circular buffer, ensuring we handle the wrap-around correctly for the first few elements.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8229990770438821770",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589646.3298275
      },
      "rev_n_6_10a587_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_10a587_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_10a587",
        "thought_text": "Construct k separate arrays where the j-th array contains elements at indices j, j+k, j+2k, ..., compute the sum for each array, and return the index corresponding to the array with the smallest sum.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "8229990770438821770",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589646.3298364
      },
      "rev_n_7_fe5bc0_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_fe5bc0_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_fe5bc0",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through all possible starting positions from 1 to k, calculate the total 'told off' power for each by summing elements at indices (start + i*k - 1) % n, and select the start position with the minimum sum.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-9011239861806343126",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589658.7348204
      },
      "rev_n_8_b1c2f9_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_b1c2f9_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_b1c2f9",
        "thought_text": "Since the array is circular and the step is k, the problem decomposes into k independent linear subarrays of length n/k; compute the sum of each subarray and find the one with the minimum total.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-9011239861806343126",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589658.7348359
      },
      "rev_n_9_a1ec2c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_a1ec2c_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_a1ec2c",
        "thought_text": "Use a sliding window approach on the expanded array (concatenated n times) to efficiently compute sums of subsequences with step k, though given constraints n <= 10^5, direct summation for k candidates is sufficient and simpler.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-9011239861806343126",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589658.7348435
      },
      "rev_n_10_6331da_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_10_6331da_0",
        "node_id": "n_10_6331da",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through each possible starting offset from 0 to k-1, calculate the total penalty by summing every k-th element in the circular array for that offset, and track the minimum sum and the smallest corresponding 1-based index.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5505844244514502483",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589673.4109905
      },
      "rev_n_11_49d80a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_49d80a_0",
        "node_id": "n_11_49d80a",
        "thought_text": "Since n is guaranteed to be divisible by k, the total penalty for any starting offset is simply the sum of a complete set of k non-overlapping subsequences spaced by k; we can precompute prefix sums or use a sliding window over the expanded array of size n+k to efficiently calculate these sums in O(n) time.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5505844244514502483",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589673.4110086
      },
      "rev_n_12_97b889_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_12_97b889_0",
        "node_id": "n_12_97b889",
        "thought_text": "Optimize the calculation by realizing that shifting the start index by 1 only changes the sum by removing one element and adding another specific element k steps away, allowing us to update the current sum in O(1) time as we iterate through all k possible starting positions.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5505844244514502483",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589673.4110165
      },
      "rev_n_13_24d4a6_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_13_24d4a6_0",
        "node_id": "n_13_24d4a6",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through all possible starting positions from 1 to k, calculate the sum of powers for the specific sequence of tasks (start, start+k, start+2k, ...) for each, and select the starting position with the minimum total power.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4308239749696365787",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589763.7729564
      },
      "rev_n_14_390b5a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_14_390b5a_0",
        "node_id": "n_14_390b5a",
        "thought_text": "Since the array is circular and n is divisible by k, we can duplicate the array to handle the wrap-around logic easily, then use a sliding window or direct summation for each of the k possible phases to find the optimal start index.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4308239749696365787",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794589763.7729723
      },
      "rev_n_15_ec40fa_0": {
